What is NOT in this repository, and why
---------------------------------------
The Amiga UNIX bootstrap `unix_boot` is **Markus Wild's** program, published on Aminet as
`misc/unix/unix_boot` version 1.1c.  It is not a Commodore product.  It is, however, derived from
Commodore's AMIX boot sources -- the author's own readme says he took them from
`/usr/sys/amiga/boot`, replaced device I/O with DOS, and converted the assembler from SGS to MIT
syntax for GCC -- so the files in it still carry

    Copyright (C) 1991, Commodore Business Machines, Inc.

No licence is stated for Markus Wild's own work.

Checked file by file, because the layering decides who could give permission for what: **all ten
source files in the archive carry Commodore's 1991 header** -- `bind.c`, `bootdata.h`,
`bootinfo.h`, `copyit.s`, `infoblock.h`, `rel.c`, `streq.c`, `streqn.c`, `Supervisor.s`,
`unix_boot.c` -- and none carries Markus Wild's name.  `unix_boot.c` heads its notice "Original
Code: Copyright (C) 1991, Commodore Business Machines, Inc.", which is the wording of someone who
modified a file and kept the attribution.  His contribution is the modifications and the
packaging; the file contents are Commodore's.  So permission from him would settle the licence of
his own work, and would not by itself make these sources redistributable.  Between the two, nothing of `unix_boot` is
redistributed here in any form beyond the diff context described below.  You must obtain the
archive yourself and run `apply.sh` against your own copy.  The same applies to the AMIX ELF
headers the build needs.

The *prebuilt* `unix_boot` in Markus Wild's archive requires `arp.library` at run time, and his
readme lists it as a requirement.  The sources do not reference it and neither does the binary
built from them here: measured, zero references to `arp.library` or `ArpBase` in `unix_boot040`
and in a fresh build, against three in the shipped executable.  The requirement therefore belongs
to the original binary, not to this.

How much of the original this repository contains -- measured, 2026-08-18
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
A unified diff reproduces the lines it removes, and the context lines around each change so that
`patch` can locate the hunk.  That is original text, and an earlier version of this NOTICE claimed
there was none of it.  Measured now:

    file           original   lines of it in this repository
    copyit.s          70           0   -- rebuilt, not patched; see below
    unix_boot.c      252          62
    rel.c            434          29
    bind.c            47           7
    Makefile          19           6

Two corrections to the 2026-08-15 table, both found while re-measuring.  It counted every line
of each patch beginning with a space or a hyphen, which also matches the `--- filename` header --
so each figure was one too high.  And it omitted `Makefile`, which is patched like the others.
The counts above exclude diff headers and hunk markers.  `unix_boot.c` rose by two genuine
context lines when a bounds check was added to the memory-region loop.

`copyit.s` was the reason the claim was false: the change replaces the 68030 MMU-disable sequence
wholesale, so a diff removes 40 of its 70 lines and carries the rest as context -- 71 of 70 lines
at three lines of context, 60 at one, 40 at zero.  No diff setting fixes that.

It is therefore **not a patch any more**.  `transforms/transform_copyit.py` rebuilds the file:
the script holds our own text and the line numbers of what survives, and reads those surviving
lines out of your copy.  It verifies the input's sha256 before writing anything and the result's
sha256 after, so a wrong input fails loudly instead of silently keeping the wrong lines.
Verified end to end: run against a clean extraction it reproduces the previous patched file
byte for byte, and the loader builds to the same binary.

Two lines of the original remain in that script, both inside sequences we wrote:
`movew #0x2700,sr`, which is the only way to mask interrupts, and a PC-relative `lea` of a data
label the file keeps.  Single instructions dictated by the machine and by the surrounding code,
recorded here rather than left for a reader to find.

`unix_boot.c` was 129 lines, half the file, until the diff context was narrowed from three lines
to one on 2026-08-15; verified the same way, byte-identical output from a clean extraction.

The patches remain useless without the original sources, which you supply.

A note on the model, because it is not ours
--------------------------------------------
The original author faced the same question and answered it the same way.  `README.BEFORE.BUILD!`
in his archive says:

    Since I didn't want to risk any Copyright war with AT&T, I didn't include the following
    files into the include/ directory ... Just copy them from your SVR4 partition back into
    this tree.

He left AT&T's ELF headers out and told the reader to supply them from their own installation.
That is exactly the arrangement this repository uses, arrived at independently and then found to
have a precedent from the person who wrote the thing being patched.

Two differences are worth stating plainly.  He was cautious about AT&T's headers while
redistributing Commodore's boot sources, which says something about what was thought settled at
the time rather than that it was.  And this repository is stricter than his: it ships patches
rather than sources, so the reader supplies not only the AT&T headers but the loader itself.

Hardware and architecture references
------------------------------------
The 68030/68040/68060 MMU-disable sequences in `transforms/transform_copyit.py` were written
from Motorola's
MC68040 and MC68060 user manuals.  No code was copied from any third-party operating system.
